WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.950
A Component diagram describes software structure and the
services or infrastructure that connect it.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:15.445
It is useful when an audience needs to understand
responsibilities and dependencies without reading a
sequence of individual messages.

00:00:15.565 --> 00:00:21.290
Our portal has a web app, API, request database,
notification queue and worker.

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:29.485
We will create another component, inspect infrastructure
kinds and interfaces, group the architecture, and apply a
shared style.

00:00:29.605 --> 00:00:35.105
State what a connection means rather than treating every
arrow as an interchangeable line.

00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:43.558
Choose Component or infrastructure from Insert and add an
Audit service.

00:00:43.678 --> 00:00:53.028
Review the kind options: the editor also supports
interfaces, databases, queues, clouds, nodes, artifacts,
files, folders and rectangles.

00:00:53.148 --> 00:00:56.873
Choose the kind for what the object represents in this
view.

00:00:56.993 --> 00:01:02.418
A database or deployment node is not the same concept as a
software component.

00:01:02.538 --> 00:01:09.463
Inspect the resulting declaration and give the object a
stable alias when other statements will refer to it.

00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:16.317
Select API and open its inspector.

00:01:16.437 --> 00:01:24.162
Kind, name and alias identify the object, while stereotype
and color describe presentation or classification.

00:01:24.282 --> 00:01:28.082
Container placement groups it within a package or node.

00:01:28.202 --> 00:01:34.277
These fields answer different questions, so avoid changing
them all merely to make the drawing fit.

00:01:34.397 --> 00:01:39.147
Inspect the source after an identity change and verify the
connections.

00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:48.567
The same common editor pattern applies here as in Class
diagrams, but the modeled meaning is architectural rather
than a member-level type design.

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:57.242
The reference example adds a Request service interface
beside API.

00:01:57.362 --> 00:02:05.237
An interface helps explain what another component relies
on, while the API implementation remains a separate
object.

00:02:05.357 --> 00:02:11.607
Read the declaration and connection together, and decide
how much detail is useful for this audience.

00:02:11.727 --> 00:02:17.402
Do not imply an implemented protocol simply because two
objects are next to each other.

00:02:17.522 --> 00:02:25.497
Use a label or note to state the relevant interaction or
contract, and keep that explanation consistent with the
Sequence model.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.050
Open Relationship to inspect the supported kinds and
endpoint controls.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:43.920
In our example, Web uses API over HTTPS, API reads and
writes the database, and notifications move through the
queue to the worker.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.390
Those labels explain different interactions.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:54.135
Reconnect an incorrectly chosen endpoint rather than
leaving a misleading architectural dependency.

00:02:54.255 --> 00:03:01.680
Check the source after reordering or moving an object into
a container so that the intended structure remains intact.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.350
A package or node groups related objects, but the kind of
grouping matters.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:17.620
The extended example puts the web app in a browser
environment and records an assumption at the API boundary.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:23.965
Compare these declarations with the preview, then inspect
package creation and Diagram settings.

00:03:24.085 --> 00:03:29.710
A deployment node, ownership grouping and logical package
can answer different questions.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:37.755
Keep a boundary explicit enough that a reader does not
have to guess whether it means physical location,
ownership or software structure.

00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:46.342
Select Web and API together and inspect the bulk styling
controls.

00:03:46.462 --> 00:03:51.087
Apply one shared style to distinguish a category of
software objects.

00:03:51.207 --> 00:03:56.732
Styling should support names and boundaries, not become
the only way to understand them.

00:03:56.852 --> 00:04:04.602
Review the source after the bulk change and check copied
or duplicated objects for unintended identifiers or
connections.

00:04:04.722 --> 00:04:11.022
The source history provides a way to reconsider the
operation when the selected set was too broad.

00:04:14.533 --> 00:04:17.158
Pause and add an Audit service.

00:04:17.278 --> 00:04:28.228
Identify the request database, notification queue and
worker as distinct responsibilities, then explain the path
from request creation to notification processing.

00:04:28.348 --> 00:04:32.873
Add one interface and one boundary that have a clear
meaning in your model.

00:04:32.993 --> 00:04:38.643
Inspect a connection, add an explanatory note and style a
verified pair of components.

00:04:38.763 --> 00:04:46.788
A successful result can be explained without pretending
that this structural diagram is a complete timeline of
runtime behavior.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:56.525
Components, infrastructure, interfaces and boundaries help
explain the system's major structure.

00:04:56.645 --> 00:05:02.620
Use messages or workflow models for the detailed behavior
that this diagram does not show.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:09.240
Keep aliases, endpoint meaning and grouping deliberate,
and use notes to state relevant assumptions.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:20.160
The next lesson models a request intake workflow with
decisions, loops and parallel work, moving from structural
responsibilities back to the order and conditions of
behavior.
